class Solution {
  public int tallerKeycapsTotalReceipt(int[] readings) {
    int[] a=readings; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {int[]distance=new int[a.length];Deque<Integer>stack=new ArrayDeque<>();for(int i=0;i<a.length;i++){while(!stack.isEmpty()&&a[stack.peek()]<a[i]){int j=stack.pop();distance[j]=i-j;}stack.push(i);}for(int x:distance)out.add(x);}
    int answer=0;for(int x:out)answer+=x;return answer;
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
Build stepsToTaller

For each number in `readings`, look to its right. Find the first number that is larger, not equal. Record how many places to the right it is. Record 0 if no larger number is found. Keep the answers in input order.

Return the answer

Add up every number in `stepsToTaller`. Store the sum in `total`. Complete `tallerKeycapsTotalReceipt` so it returns `total`. Return it, do not print it.

Why this works

Each value in `stepsToTaller` follows task 1. The later tasks use only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(n); extra memory, including the helper list, O(n). n is the input length. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

